### Drill 2: Multi-Replica Shared Storage (`ReadWriteMany`) (`01-nfs/`)

A fundamental CKA exam competency is understanding storage access modes:

- **`ReadWriteOnce` (RWO)**: Volume can be mounted as read-write by a single node.
- **`ReadOnlyMany` (ROX)**: Volume can be mounted as read-only by multiple nodes simultaneously.
- **`ReadWriteMany` (RWX)**: Volume can be mounted as read-write by multiple nodes simultaneously.

#### The Multi-Attach Exam Trap

If a multi-replica Deployment uses block storage (such as AWS EBS, Ceph RBD, or local disks) with `ReadWriteOnce`,
pods scheduled across different worker nodes will fail to start with `Multi-Attach error for volume`. Network
filesystems like NFS natively support `ReadWriteMany`, enabling distributed pods across `kube-worker-1` and
`kube-worker-2` to share live data.

#### Step 1: Imperatively Generate the Deployment Skeleton

```bash
cd {{ cka_lab_volumes_dir }}/01-nfs

kubectl create deployment nfs-shared-web \
  --image=nginx:alpine \
  --replicas=2 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml > nfs-shared-deployment.yaml
```

#### Step 2: Attach the NFS Volume to the Pod Template

Open `nfs-shared-deployment.yaml` in `vim` and add the volume configuration under `spec.template.spec`:

```yaml
spec:
  replicas: 2
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx:alpine
          volumeMounts:
            - name: nfs-shared-data
              mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html
      volumes:
        - name: nfs-shared-data
          nfs:
            server: {{ cka_lab_nfs_server_hostname }}
            path: {{ cka_lab_nfs_export_dir }}
```

Alternatively, review the pre-staged manifest:

```bash
cat 02-nfs-deployment-shared.yaml
kubectl apply -f 02-nfs-deployment-shared.yaml
kubectl rollout status deployment/nfs-shared-web --timeout=60s
```

#### Step 3: Verify Multi-Node Spreading and Real-Time Data Sharing

Verify that the pods are running on distinct worker nodes:

```bash
kubectl get pods -l app=nfs-shared-web -o wide
```

Prove that a file written by one replica is immediately visible to the other:

```bash
POD1=$(kubectl get pods -l app=nfs-shared-web -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
POD2=$(kubectl get pods -l app=nfs-shared-web -o jsonpath='{.items[1].metadata.name}')

# Write from first replica
kubectl exec "${POD1}" -- sh -c 'echo "Shared write from '"${POD1}"' at $(date)" > /usr/share/nginx/html/replica-test.txt'

# Verify read from second replica
kubectl exec "${POD2}" -- cat /usr/share/nginx/html/replica-test.txt
```

